WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.970
Speaker 1: There is a new report, an extremely
disturbing report which found that at least

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:13.280
eight Republicans who were at the January
6th rally have been elected to office.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:20.180
Now these are not necessarily people who were
participating in the riot, but these are folks

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.199
who were at the rally on January 6th Huffington
Post article.

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:27.929
At least eight Republicans who were elected
at the rally just got elected to office, and

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:32.600
even more attendees of the rally that turned
into the riot will likely be on the ballot

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.390
in 2022.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:40.170
Although most have claimed they didn't breach
the U.S. Capitol on that day, all were participants

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.250
in the demonstration leading up to the attack.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:48.250
Standing alongside extremists to take part
in the finale of a months long anti-democratic

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:54.481
campaign to falsely claim that then-President
Donald Trump didn't really lose 2020, their

00:00:54.481 --> 00:00:57.970
victories on Tuesday are a possible sign of
things to come.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:04.080
HuffPost previously identified 57 state and
local Republican officials who attended the

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.000
rally, many of whom will be up for reelection
and likely will win next year.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.960
That these candidates enjoy the support of
the wider Republican Party and are winning

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.820
elections does not bode well for American
democracy.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.990
That's an understatement, showing that one
of the country's major political parties.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:25.050
Despite some initial gestures at being horrified
by the events of Jan. six, is almost completely

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:29.710
unrepentant over its role in fomenting the
historic attack on the Capitol.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:30.710
And why wouldn't they?

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:36.780
There have barely been consequences, as I
said before, not only barely consequences

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.640
for the perpetrators, many of their cases
still pending, but no real consequences for

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.090
the insiders, and some of them are getting
rewarded by getting elected.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:52.510
Among the Jan. six attendees who won office
on Tuesday were two Republicans reelected

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:57.229
to the Virginia House of Delegates Dave Lerach
and John Maguire.

00:01:57.229 --> 00:02:02.130
Marie Mar, a restaurant owner who bragged
about her attendance at the Stop the Steal

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:07.050
rally and who in a since deleted Facebook
post, warned of a coming civil war in which

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:10.640
she would be willing to fight and die for
both her family and small businesses also

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.190
want a seat in the Virginia House of Delegates
on Tuesday.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:19.090
In city councils across the country, rally
attendee Natalie Jiang Gula won a seat in

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.110
Nampa, Idaho.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:28.691
Christine Eade, who didn't go into the Capitol
and later blamed the violence on Antifa, won

00:02:28.691 --> 00:02:31.660
a seat in Watchung New Jersey.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:37.050
Charles Asperger also won a seat on the Town
Council of Mansfield, Connecticut.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:43.090
Susan Solloway organized transportation to
the rally won reelection to the Hunterdon

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.260
County, New Jersey board of directors in Braintree,
Massachusetts.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.490
A former high school teacher who resigned
his position after local activists sent a

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.819
photo of him outside the Capitol won a seat
on the local school committee.

00:02:54.819 --> 00:02:57.360
It goes on and on and on.

